modules.detail:
This minimal recipe demonstrates mechanics, not the task's answer or layout. Replace placeholder URLs and INTERACTION_NODE_ID using the bootstrap. Write meaningful task content and use scoped stable keys. Add richer detail before author.write when preferences require it. Inspect CompletionContract.returnRequirements first; read attached-navigation and stage required links/presentation changes to this exact candidate root BEFORE Advance or Return.
import { RelayerGraphClient } from 'GRAPH_CLIENT_MODULE_URL';
const graph = RelayerGraphClient.fromEnv();
const before = await graph.getCurrent();
const author = graph.authoring('response-INTERACTION_NODE_ID');
const layer = author.layer('overview');
const answer = layer.node('answer', {icon:'workflow',title:'Meaningful finding',detail:'Completed result and relevant limitations'});
layer.layout([[answer,0.5,0.5]], {edgeShape:'default',defaultNode:answer});
const written = await author.write(layer);
if(before.currentLayerId != null) await graph.addAction(answer,{kind:'navigate',relation:'reference',label:'Previous view',target:before.currentLayerId,sourceLayer:written.rootLayer,clientKey:'prior-current'});
await graph.addAction(INTERACTION_NODE_ID,{kind:'navigate',relation:'expand',label:'Response',target:written.rootLayer,clientKey:'root-response'});
console.log(await graph.advanceCurrent(written.rootLayer,before.headRevision,'publish-response'));
Publish useful Current findings or consequential questions while working. Inspect advisory previews before final acceptance. Use finish-current after the requested work is complete. Each later Current and final root must retain navigation to the previous Current. Retarget the same root action key before each advance; give each distinct advance a new operation key and refresh Current afterward. Current acceptance makes that phase immutable: revise using a new draft snapshot, not by rewriting accepted records. Only a typed feature_disabled error permits the legacy submit-only path; other errors need repair.

When the requested task is done, outstanding drafts and all CompletionContract.returnRequirements are satisfied, and the already published Current is final, Return that exact accepted layer. Do not rerun its accepted writes:
const current = await graph.getCurrent();
if(current.lifecycle !== 'active' || current.currentLayerId == null) throw new Error('No active Current to return');
console.log(await graph.returnCurrent(current.currentLayerId,current.headRevision,'finish-current'));
If there is no published Current, author the task result first. If the final result differs, write a new coherent snapshot and advance it before returning. A checkpoint alone is neither graph acceptance nor task completion. Under the legacy feature-disabled path, graph.submit(INTERACTION_NODE_ID) validates the complete reachable response; terminal submission ends graph access. Do not retry a successful or unknown terminal outcome.

Use a NodeObject's typed detailAuthoring builder before author.write or submitNode; the node owns its detail. Example for a drafted answer node:
import { html, css } from 'GRAPH_CLIENT_MODULE_URL';
answer.detailAuthoring.setComponent('main',html`<article><h2>Task finding</h2><p>Explain the actual result here.</p></article>`,css`article {font-family:system-ui;line-height:1.6;padding:1rem;} h2 {font-size:1.4rem;}`);
Use task-specific hierarchy and visual relationships where useful. The compiler accepts a constrained HTML/CSS language; unsupported properties, tags or attributes fail before transport. Read css-reference for targeted schema checks. Each component/asset/control has a stable local key. Builder contents freeze at first submit; a draft node's accepted compiled envelope is retained for exact transport retries. Do not mutate a frozen builder or invoke an invented finalize function. After Current accepts a phase, changed content needs new draft objects. Preserve a plain detail fallback that truthfully describes the same result.

Inspect only the schema facts needed for the current design, rather than printing the whole compiler manual:
import { detailAuthoringReference } from 'GRAPH_CLIENT_MODULE_URL';
const reference = detailAuthoringReference();
console.log({wantedProperties:['display','gap','padding','border-collapse'].map(name=>({name,allowed:reference.cssProperties.includes(name)}))});
Other reference fields include elements, globalAttributes, elementAttributes, cssFunctions, cssAtRules, cssPseudoClasses, cssPseudoElements, themeSelectors and limits. Filter these fields to the specific question. Standard browser CSS is not automatically in this allowlist. If the compiler reports an unsupported property, remove or replace it with an allowed design; do not disable validation. Avoid unnecessary complex CSS for a small explanation. Read saved-program-repair after a rejected authored draft.

Respect the exact attached presentation preferences. Authored detail supports the product theme via [data-relayer-theme="light"] and [data-relayer-theme="dark"] selectors. Do not use system prefers-color-scheme as the product's theme. Give both modes readable contrast and intentional backgrounds; avoid hard-coded light-only text/background combinations. Missing theme-specific styles render the same authored presentation in both modes, not an automatic recoloring. Example:
[data-relayer-theme="light"] article { color:#182c34; background-color:#fafbf9; }
[data-relayer-theme="dark"] article { color:#edf2f3; background-color:#121b20; }
Keep content useful without color alone; use labels, text and relationships.

Create the graph action on the same drafted source node and source layer as its authored detail. Bind it using typed detailCapability, not an HTML href with an internal URL or a fabricated action ID. Example for an already scoped layer and answer:
import {html,css,detailCapability} from 'GRAPH_CLIENT_MODULE_URL';
const prior = await graph.getLayer(PRIOR_ACCEPTED_LAYER_ID);
const ref = layer.action('prior',answer,{kind:'navigate',relation:'reference',label:'Earlier explanation',target:prior.layer});
answer.detailAuthoring.setComponent('prior-link',html`<button gc=${detailCapability.reference('prior-control',ref)}>Earlier explanation</button>`,css`button {font:inherit;}`);
Use this only when that prior layer is supplied or discoverable under current scope. A mounted control must reference a real supported action with the right kind/relation, owner and source layer. Do not drop existing controls during an attached presentation change; read attached-navigation.

A response layer is a useful user-facing view, not a transcript of tool calls. Use the task's actual entities and relationships. Plain content may be one node; do not create a hierarchy only to satisfy a recipe. A layer normally contains 1..5 connected nodes. Use 6..8 only when keeping them together is clearer, passing private sizeJustification (20..500 characters) in layer.layout(placements,{edgeShape:'default',sizeJustification:'…'}). Never expose that justification in user-visible content. Split more than 8 nodes. Each visible node needs one finite normalized placement (0..1) in a versioned authored layout. For scoped authoring:
layer.layout([[queue,0.15,0.5],[worker,0.5,0.5],[results,0.85,0.5]],{edgeShape:'default',defaultNode:queue});
layer.edge('queue-worker',queue,worker);
layer.edge('worker-results',worker,results);
Edges are undirected, within-layer relationships, without self-loops or duplicate endpoint pairs. Layout a connected, readable view when the task has connections. A node-level action belongs to a layer that contains its source node. Read navigation-expand for useful child views or navigation-reference for already accepted views. Do not invent actions merely to make discarded drafts reachable.

Reference actions navigate to an already accepted layer without authoring it again. Read only the scoped layer supplied by input or found through task-search. With scoped draft objects:
const earlier = await graph.getLayer(PRIOR_ACCEPTED_LAYER_ID);
layer.action('earlier',answer,{kind:'navigate',relation:'reference',label:'Earlier explanation',target:earlier.layer});
References do not grant write authority. The reference target's accepted graph, details, controls and navigation remain intact. Keep labels helpful to the user, not internal API names. Only use an expand when owning a fresh authored child closure. Cross-reference cycles are different from forbidden expansion cycles. If attaching the navigation to existing accepted content, follow attached-navigation and its explicit contract authority.

graph.search uses the existing accepted-graph query engine within the completion's read permit. It never grants project/thread access. Query-v1 supports exact/comparative property filters and bounded paths, not natural-language, substring, regex, full-text or vector retrieval. Put values in tagged parameters:
const hits=await graph.search({queryContractVersion:1,query:'MATCH (n:Content) WHERE n.title = $title RETURN n.title AS title',parameters:{title:{type:'string',value:'Queue'}}});
Use supplied graph pointers directly when sufficient. Inspect truncated on every result; default rows=5, hard rows=8 and full envelope=16KiB. Do not return full large detail when metadata will do. Integer query values are signed 64-bit decimal strings. Only Content kind/icon/title/detail/state and Layer state/layout_version are visible properties; n.id is not a public property. Prefer exact manifest prerequisites for instruction dependencies, not generic graph neighbors.

Instruction search is structured lookup over this pinned corpus, not semantic search. The metadata directory in the bootstrap already supplies exact titles, keys, node IDs and prerequisites. Choose relevant topics from it; a direct reasoned read is often sufficient. For graph search use an exact catalog title and a short operational reason:
await graph.searchInstructions({queryContractVersion:1,query:'MATCH (n:Content) WHERE n.title = $title RETURN n.title AS title',parameters:{title:{type:'string',value:'Check supported CSS properties and elements'}},reason:{purpose:'search',detail:'Locate CSS property validation guidance'}});
V2 search returns only title/kind/state/icon projections, no bodies or whole nodes. Fetch selected bodies through retrieveInstructions with a reason. Query-v1 lacks substring, case folding, synonym expansion and ranked relevance. Zero hits do not prove absence of relevant guidance. Read the trusted manifest directory and broaden the topic choice; generic CONNECTED neighbors mix directory order and dependencies. Manifest prerequisites are authoritative.

When a saved-program graph call rejects an unaccepted draft, inspect the actual natural-language/compiler issues. Fix the smallest rejected part and rerun the saved program rather than resending a large rewritten script:
import {rerunGraphProgram} from 'GRAPH_CLIENT_MODULE_URL';
await rerunGraphProgram('PROGRAM_ID',[{find:'exact rejected text',replace:'corrected text'}]);
The launcher prints each program's real ID. Reuse stable client keys and keep each identity-owning node/layer/action scope unchanged. Rerun only unaccepted phases; accepted Current cannot be rewritten. Do not repeat unrelated workspace side effects. Read Current before repairing a program that may already have published; skip accepted writes and repair only remaining drafts. A successful or ambiguous terminal result must not be rerun; trusted supervision owns recovery.
